    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I will provide a technical explanation, focusing on the mathematical and physical mechanism.\n\nThe Kutta condition is a requirement for the smooth flow of fluid over a surface, including an airfoil. Mathematically, it is expressed as a continuity equation at the trailing edge of the airfoil. The Kutta condition states that the flow must be continuous and smooth across the trailing edge, with no abrupt changes in velocity or pressure.\n\nTo satisfy the Kutta condition, the flow must have a circulation ($\\Gamma$) around the airfoil. Circulation is a measure of the rotational motion of the fluid around the airfoil. The mathematical requirement for circulation can be derived from the potential flow equations, which describe the flow of an inviscid fluid.\n\nThe potential flow equations are based on the Laplace equation, which describes the velocity potential ($\\phi$) of the fluid. The velocity potential is related to the velocity ($\\mathbf{u}$) of the fluid by the equation:\n\n$\\mathbf{u} = \\nabla \\phi$\n\nThe Kutta condition can be expressed mathematically as:\n\n$\\frac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial s} = 0$ at $s = a$\n\nwhere $s$ is",
